= How movement works

Every technique in this book solves one of a few mechanical problems. How do you keep your weight over your feet? How do you hang without wasting strength? How do you stop your body rotating off the wall? How do you put your body where a hold wants it? Once you understand these ideas, the named techniques stop looking like a long list. You can predict which one a problem needs.

== Centre of gravity and base of support

Your #ix("Centre of gravity", main: true)#jt("centre of gravity") is the point your weight acts through. When you stand upright, it sits in your pelvis, near your navel #src("B16"), but it moves when you bend, reach or raise a leg. Gravity pulls straight down through it. Picture a #jt("plumb line") hanging from it: the line shows at a glance where your weight goes.

Your #ix("Base of support", main: true)#jt("base of support") is the set of points holding you: footholds, handholds, a knee or a heel. On the ground it is the area between your feet. On a wall, ask where the plumb line~falls:

- If the plumb line falls *over or between your footholds*, your feet carry your weight and your hands only stop you falling backwards, which costs little grip #src("B8", "B17").
- If the plumb line falls *outside your feet*, your fingers must hold you up and in #src("B7", "B8"). Every second in that position costs strength.

The steeper the wall, the more weight moves from your feet to your hands. Measurements show this even between a slight slab and a slight overhang #src("B8"). So the first question for any position is: _where is my weight, and what carries it?_ Most footwork and body-positioning techniques move your hips so the answer is "my feet".

#figure(image("../figures/concept-balance.svg"), caption: [(a) Hips centred between the feet: both feet share the weight. (b) Moving the hips over the left foot puts all the weight on it. The right foot can then move without the hands taking over.], kind: image, supplement: [Figure]) <fig-balance>

Good climbers share a rhythm: *shift the hips first, then move the limb*#ix("Hips!shifting first") #src("B22"). Before you lift a foot, move your hips over the other foot. Before you reach, move your hips under or beside the hold you reach from, so the reach doesn't drag your weight off your feet. Expert climbers shift their weight from side to side more than novices do#ix("Experts and novices!weight shifting") #src("B9"). #tref("weight-shift"), #tref("precise-footwork"), #tref("foot-swap") and #tref("slab", body: [slab climbing]) all depend on this.

#filmstrip("filmstrip-hips-first.svg", [Hips first, then the foot], [Watch the plumb line: the free foot moves only while the line falls on the other foot.])

== Straight arms: hang on your skeleton

Muscle holds a bent arm bent. Lock your elbow at ninety degrees and your biceps and shoulder muscles work all the time #src("G71"). Straighten the arm and your elbow muscles can rest #src("B12", "B30"). Coaches talk about hanging on your skeleton #src("B12", "B22"), but as a physiotherapist points out, you always are: the saving comes from using fewer muscles and using them less hard #src("B50"). Your shoulder#ix("Shoulders!on straight arms") muscles must still hold the joint in place #src("B50"). So learn #tref("straight-arms") early (this book's suggestion). The UK's coach training lists them as a way to find a rest #src("B1").

On long problems your forearms #jt("pump")#ix("Pump!reducing"): they swell, ache and weaken. Coaches recommend hanging on straight arms whenever you aren't pulling, to ease the pump #src("B22", "B36"), but we found no study measuring this. The closest study tested 12-second hangs with the feet off the wall: the biceps and shoulder muscles worked harder on bent arms, while the one forearm muscle it tested, a finger flexor, showed no change #src("G71"). It measured no pump, so it says little about a real climb (this book's reading). Straight arms also force you to move with your legs, because a straight arm can't pull.

#figure(image("../figures/concept-straight-arms.svg"), caption: [(a) Bent arms on an overhang: the arm muscles hold the body up. (b) Straight arms, bent legs: the elbow muscles rest while the legs push.], kind: image, supplement: [Figure])

Straight arms are not a rule for every moment. You bend your arms to pull through a move or to #tref("lock-off", body: [lock off]). On steep ground the skill is to straighten them again as soon as the move is done. Then set up the next move with straight arms: move the feet, turn the hips.

== Hips and the wall

How far your hips#ix("Hips!distance from the wall") are from the wall decides how much weight goes through your feet. The right distance depends on the wall angle.

- *On an overhang*#ix("Overhangs!hip position"), your feet are below and in front of you, while gravity pulls your hips away from the wall. The further they sag, the more your feet slide off and the more your arms carry #src("B7", "B16"). Bring your hips in, square or with one hip to the wall, to move your weight towards your toes #src("B28"). Pulling with your toes helps too: both press your feet onto the holds. See #tref("hip-turn"), #tref("toe-pull") and #tref("overhang").
- *On a vertical wall*#ix("Vertical walls!body position"), stand tall, with your body straight and over your feet #src("B17"). Don't sink low to straighten your arms: it moves weight off your feet onto your fingers #src("B8").
- *On a slab*#ix("Slab technique!hip position"), where the wall leans away from you, hugging it pushes your feet off the holds. Stand straight over your feet with your hips slightly *away* from the wall, so your weight presses down through your shoes #src("B17"). See #tref("slab").

"Hips in" does not mean "as tight as you can". Pulling in too tight on an overhang blocks your turn and unloads your feet #src("B48"). On a test wall, expert climbers held their centre of gravity slightly farther from the wall than novices did#ix("Experts and novices!distance from the wall") #src("B9").

#figure(image("../figures/concept-hips-in.svg"), caption: [On a 30° overhang. (a) Hips out: the plumb line falls far from the feet, so the arms carry the weight. (b) Hips in: the weight moves towards the feet, which stay on.], kind: image, supplement: [Figure])

== Holds have a direction

A hold is not just "good" or "bad". It has a surface you can pull on, which only works when you pull away from the way it faces. A #tref("jug", body: [jug]) works from below. An #tref("undercling", body: [undercling]) works from above, pulling upward. A #tref("sidepull", body: [sidepull]) whose gripping edge faces left works when your body is to its right, leaning away from it. Used with your body on its left, the same hold becomes a #tref("gaston", body: [gaston]). A #tref("sloper", body: [sloper]) works only when your body hangs below it, arm straight #src("B31"). So the holds dictate where your body goes. #ix("Direction of pull", main: true)

This is the most useful idea for #ix("Route reading")reading problems. Look at which way every hold faces, then ask where your body must be for each hold to work. Chapter 2 turns this into a method, and Chapter 5 describes each hold type.

Three kinds of force come out of this:

- *Pulling*, towards you or downward, as on a jug.
- *Pushing*, as when you press a palm on a #tref("volume", body: [volume]) or #tref("mantle", body: [mantle]) onto a ledge.
- *Opposition*: two forces pushing or pulling against each other. Two hands squeeze a block (#tref("compression", inner: true)). A hand pulls one way while a foot pushes the other (#tref("layback", inner: true)). Your feet push apart in a corner (#tref("stemming", inner: true)). With opposition, holds that are useless on their own start to work #src("B1", "B14"). See #tref("opposition").

